od,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fe1267556-7915-4e27-9ce0-ac64817c76e8_1080x1080.jpeg 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!Vpbx!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fe1267556-7915-4e27-9ce0-ac64817c76e8_1080x1080.jpeg 1456w\" sizes=\"100vw\" loading=\"lazy\"></picture><div class=\"image-link-expand\"><div class=\"pencraft pc-display-flex pc-gap-8 pc-reset\"><button tabindex=\"0\" type=\"button\" class=\"pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container restack-image buttonBase-GK1x3M\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\" viewBox=\"0 0 20 20\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke=\"var(--color-fg-primary)\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" class=\"icon-noB79L\"><g><path d=\"M2.53001 7.81595C3.49179 4.73911 6.43281 2.5 9.91173 2.5C13.1684 2.5 15.9537 4.46214 17.0852 7.23684L17.6179 8.67647M17.6179 8.67647L18.5002 4.26471M17.6179 8.67647L13.6473 6.91176M17.4995 12.1841C16.5378 15.2609 13.5967 17.5 10.1178 17.5C6.86118 17.5 4.07589 15.5379 2.94432 12.7632L2.41165 11.3235M2.41165 11.3235L1.5293 15.7353M2.41165 11.3235L6.38224 13.0882\"></path></g></svg></button><button tabindex=\"0\" type=\"button\" class=\"pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container view-image buttonBase-GK1x3M\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" class=\"lucide lucide-maximize2 lucide-maximize-2 icon-noB79L\"><polyline points=\"15 3 21 3 21 9\"></polyline><polyline points=\"9 21 3 21 3 15\"></polyline><line x1=\"21\" x2=\"14\" y1=\"3\" y2=\"10\"></line><line x1=\"3\" x2=\"10\" y1=\"21\" y2=\"14\"></line></svg></button></div></div></div></a></figure></div><p>Have a chat with Erin and Subba on the retreat program and details.</p><p class=\"button-wrapper\" data-attrs=\"{&quot;url&quot;:&quot;https://beingme.com.sg/power-and-pause&quot;,&quot;text&quot;:&quot;Arrange a Personal Chat&quot;,&quot;action&quot;:null,&quot;class&quot;:&quot;button-wrapper&quot;}\" data-component-name=\"ButtonCreateButton\"><a class=\"button primary button-wrapper\" href=\"https://beingme.com.sg/power-and-pause\"><span>Arrange a Personal Chat</span></a></p><div><hr></div>",
  "body_text": "Think about something that irritated you recently — perhaps not a deep wound, just a tone in a message or a decision made without you. \n\nNotice how readily the mind returns to it, and starts obsessing over it, even now.\n\nThe Dhammapada is interested in exactly that kind of fixation. \n\nThe text from this ancient collection of teachings doesn’t deny that something happened to us. It points at what the mind does afterwards — in the endless replaying and rehearsing. \n\nWhen a story is repeated enough, it can stop feeling like a memory and start to feel like a fact about who we are. This identification is where suffering sets in, rather than in the event itself. \n\n“Hatred never ends through hatred. By non-hate alone does it end,” says the teachings. \n\nNon-hate here doesn’t equate to love. The practice doesn’t ask you to feel affection towards someone who hurt you, nor is forgiveness the entry fee. \n\nNon-hate sits closer with equanimity — a refusal to swing to extremes, and a choice of not picking at our own wounds. \n\nThe Dhammapada draws a useful distinction between someone who works skilfully with anger — called a charioteer, and everyone else — merely a rein-holder: composed on the outside but still seething underneath. \n\nSuppression can easily be mistaken as having control over the emotion.\n\nThe freedom beyond anger being described here isn’t tolerance, and it isn’t passivity in the face of harm either. \n\nIt’s the capacity to meet a person or situation we don’t like with greater ease. \n\nIt is the ability to enjoy peace of mind without being dependent on someone else changing.\n\n---\n\nAt Take A Pause Live on Insight Timer over 1-2 August 2026, we practiced watching anger show up in the body and mind, moment by moment, with awareness and equanimity. We closed the session with a reading from Erin Lee’s Take A Pause: Waking up to a life of clarity.\n\nMeditation Practice\n\nTap Play to Practice Together:\n\nThis post is free for some time, and then archived for paid subscribers to support a sustainable, long-term sharing of mindful content. Upgrade to receive lifetime access to the full archive and ongoing updates.\n\nShare\n\n---\n\nThe Wholehearted Woman Retreat\n\nA five-day mindfulness retreat into quiet wisdom and the light within\n\n1-5 October 2026\nSiem Reap, Cambodia\n\n*Early Bird Offer till 31 August 2026\n\nLed by Erin Lee\n\nJoin Erin at Angkor Wat\n\n---\n\nPower & Pause Retreat\n\nA high-touch program supported by structured processes, guided practices, and reflective assignments\n\n8 - 12 October 2026\nUbud, Bali\n\nLed by Subba Sattvic and Erin Lee\n\nHave a chat with Erin and Subba on the retreat program and details.\n\nArrange a Personal Chat\n\n---",
